  Your answers pinned down most of the design. The one axis genuinely still open is [1mthe stored data model[0m — what
  actually sits in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m — and it's the choice the rest of the module is written around.

  [1mApproach A — bounded append-only event log.[0m One key holding an array of event records, newest appended, capped at a
  fixed count (say 500) with oldest dropped on overflow. [3mTrade-off:[0m you get real history — sequence, timestamps, repeat
  logins — and the shape is uniform for whatever future forms push into it. Costs a cap you have to pick, and the whole
  array is read and rewritten on each append (fine at this size, not at 50k events). [3mComplexity: moderate.[0m

  [1mApproach B — last-state-per-event record.[0m An object keyed by event name, each holding only the most recent occurrence
  plus a count. [3mTrade-off:[0m tiny, can never grow unbounded, and no retention policy to design. But it answers only "who
  logged in most recently, how many times" — the sequence is gone, so you can't ever ask "did this user log in before
  that failure." [3mComplexity: trivial.[0m

  [1mApproach C — session log plus durable rollup.[0m Two tiers: a full event list scoped to the current session, and a small
  persistent summary that survives it. [3mTrade-off:[0m bounded growth with detail where you usually want it. But it's two
  storage shapes, two lifecycles, and two sets of tests for a subsystem whose first consumer is one login form.
  [3mComplexity: high.[0m

  [1mRecommendation: A.[0m "Track who logged in" is a question about history, and B can't answer it — the moment you want to
  know [3mwhen[0m or [3mhow often[0m, B needs replacing rather than extending. C is real engineering, but it's buying a problem you
  don't have yet at triple the surface area. A's cap is the one thing you have to choose, and choosing it wrong is a
  one-constant fix.
